  "text_md": "![](<11_pg421_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<11_pg421_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nTUB 8g3 TUG\n\n- (2) The top of a malt-kiln. Essex.\n- (3) The gurnet. Cornw.\n- (4) One mode of curing the lues venerea was by the tub, the patient sweating for a considerable time in a heated tub. This mode is often alluded to by early writers.\n\n\nTRUNK-WEAM. A fiddle.\n\nTRUNLIN. A large coal. North.\n\nTRUNNLE. The same as Trendle, q. v.\n\nTRUNTLEMENT. Trumpery. North.\n\nTRUPHILLE. A trifle. TRUSH. (1) A hassock. Kent.\n\nTUBBAN. A clod of earth. Cornw. TUBBER. A cooper. North. TUBBLE. A mattock. Devon. TUB-IRON. An iron placed in front of a smith's\n\n- (2) To trush about, to htter.\n- (3) To run about in the dirt. North. TRUSLE. (1) Trust. Weber.\n\n\n(2) To wrap up ; to get ready. TRUSS. (1) A padded jacket worn under the\n\nfire-place, having a hole through which the spout of the bellows is put. TUBLE. Earthenware. West.\n\narmour to protect the skin.\n\n- (2) To tie the points of hose. To truss up, to tuck up the gown, &c.\n- (3) The baggage of an army.\n- (4) To pack up. Hence, to make ready. And tritsse al that he mithen fynde Of hise, in arke, or in kiste.\n\nHavelok, 2018.\n\n- (5) A boy's game, like leap-frog.\n- (6) Truss up, to hang a person. TRUSSEL. (1) A pack, or bundle.\n\n\nTU-BRUGGE. A drawbridge.\n\nTUCK. (1) To eat. Also, an appetite.\n\n- (2) A short pinafore. East.\n- (3) To smart with pain. Wilts. In Devonshire, to pinch severely.\n- (4) A slap. Devon.\n- (5) A horizontal fold made in a garment to accommodate it to the height of a growing person.\n- (6) To touch. Somerset.\n- (7) A rapier. Still in use.\n- (8) To chuck. Cornw. TUCKER. (1) A fuller. West.\n\n\n(2) A stand for a barrel. Kent.\n\nTRUSSES. The same as Troitw*, q. V.\n\nTRUSSING. In falconry, is a hawk's raising any fowl or prey aloft ; soaring up, and then descending with it to the ground.\n\n(2) The same as Pinner, q. v. TUCKER-IN. A chambermaid. West. TUCKET. A slight flourish on a trumpet. TUCKING. A bag used for carrying beans in\n\nTRUSSING-BASKET. A basket used for conveying large parcels of goods. Called also a\n\ntrussing-coffer.\n\nTRUSSING-BED. A travelling bed. \"Trussyng\n\nwhen setting them. Glouc.\n\nTUCKING-GIRDLE. \" Tuckyng kyrdell, samc-\n\nbedde, lit de champ,\" Palsgrave.\n\nAlso my large bed of black velvet, embroidered with a circle of fetter-locks, and garters, all the beds made for my body called in England trussing beds.\n\nture decourser,\" Palsgrave.\n\nTUCKS. Iron pins in the frame of a timber-\n\ntug to prevent the timber slipping off.\n\nTeat. Vetusl. p. 141.\n\nTUCKSHELLS. Tusks. Sussex.\n\nTRUT. (1) Stercus. Heame.\n\nTUE. (1) To rumple. North.\n\n(2) The cry of hunters returning home after the\n\n(2) The same as I'ew, q. v.\n\nsport is finished.\n\nTUEL. (1) A towel. West.\n\nTRUTHY. Faithful ; veracious. East.\n\n- (2) The fundament. See Tewel.\n- (3) A vexatious meddling. North. TUEN. To go.\n\n\nTRY. (1) To fare. Somerset.\n\n- (2) A corn screen. Also, to screen.\n- (3) To boil down lard. East.\n- (4) How de try, how do you do ? Ea^m.\n- (5) A club tipped with iron. TRYALYTES. Three benefices united. TRYERS.\n\n\nAnt alle the other that mine buen,\n\nShule to blisse with me tuen.\n\nHarrowing of Hell, p. 29.\n\nTUFF. (1) A Turkish turban.\n\n- (2) A tassel. Also, to ornament with tassels.\n- (3) A lock of wool.\n- (4) To spit or hiss, as a cat. TUFFOLD. A small outhouse. YorJcsh. TUFT; A grove, or plantation. TUFT-HUNTER. A hanger-on to noblemen\n\n\nAnd shew'd themselves as errant lyars.\n\nAs th' were 'prentice to the tryers.\n\nBronte's Songs, 1661, p. 167.\n\nTRYSTI. Trusty ; secure.\n\nOn trt/sti roche heo stondeth fast. And wyth depe dyche buth all be cast.\n\nand persons of quality.\n\nReligious Poems, xv. Cent.\n\nTUFT-MOCKADO. A mixed stuff-made to imi-\n\nTRYVE. To drive.\n\ntate tufted taffeta, or velvet.\n\nIn chastisynge hath made a rod To tryveawey hire wantonnesse.\n\nTUFT-TAFFATY. A taffaty tufted, or left with a nap on it, like velvet. Sleeveless his jerkin was, and it had been Velvet, but 'twas now (so much ground was seen) Become tufftaffaty ; and our children shall See it plain rash a while, then nought at all.\n\nGower, MS. Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 111. TU. To work hard. North.\n\nTUARN. The place in an iron furnace which\n\nreceives the metal. StaJ''.\n\nTUAY. Two.\n\nDonne's Poems, p. 129.\n\nFrom arnemorwe to the midday.\n\nTUG. (1) A contest. Var. dial.\n\nHe hadde strengthe of knightes tuay.\n\n- (2) A timber-carriage. Sussex.\n- (3) To rob ; to spoil. North.\n- (4) A difficult undertaking. West.\n\n\nArthour and Merlin, p. 178.\n\nTUB. (1) Tale of a tub, a stupid nonsensical\n\nstory.\n\n",
